Step 1: Find the target file
Use this to find the directory where the target file is stored while hiding permission errors.

Bash
find / -type f -name "filename" 2>/dev/null

Step 2: Copy the Hash
View the file and copy the hash string.

Bash
cat filename

Step 3: Setup (Local v Remote)
If you are on an SSH server, copy the hash to your local machine (Parrot/Kali) since that is where your tools are installed.

To downoad - scp USER@IP ADDRESS:"PATH" (YOUR DOWNLOADING PATH)


Step 4: Create the hash file
Create the file in your home directory to avoid path/permission issues. Using echo -n is important so you don't add a hidden newline character.

Bash
echo -n "hash_content_here" > hash.txt

Step 5: Identify the Hash Type
Run the hash through an identifier to find the format (MD5, SHA-1, MySQL5, etc).

Bash
hash-identifier

Step 6: Cracking
Choose the command based on the identified format:

Auto-Detect: john hash.txt
MD5: john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=rockyou.txt hash.txt
SHA-1: john --format=raw-sha1 --wordlist=rockyou.txt hash.txt
MySQL5: john --format=mysql-sha1 --wordlist=rockyou.txt hash.txt
SHA-256: john soh.txt --format=raw-sha256 --wordlist=/home/i5hubh4m/Pictures/ww.mnf

View Results: If John says "No hashes left to crack," it's already solved. Use this to see it:

Bash
john --show hash.txt

Wordlist Setup
Wordlists are usually stored in /usr/share/wordlists/. You need to unzip them before use.

Find the list:
Bash
ls -l /usr/share/wordlists/

Unzip it:
Bash
cp /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt.gz .
gunzip rockyou.txt.gz

